From: shashidaren <16284436+shashidaren@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:44:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix the first autosync run: check_data false positive,
 event-log bloat

Two alarms from the 2026-10-19 first run (docs/autosync_first_run_2026-10-19.md).

check_data: chain only the current book.
  Both books were reset to $300 on 2026-10-04 with `paper.py --reset`, which
  zeroes the balance but does not rewrite trades.jsonl, so the file holds one
  permanent legitimate break per book - exactly the "2 fail" that was reported.
  The chain is now checked over the last `closed` exits from paper_account.json,
  and the counters are proven to describe that same set. A break inside the
  current book, a state/ledger disagreement, and closed > logged exits are still
  hard failures. tests/test_check_data.py covers both directions.

Publish ledger.jsonl, not trades.jsonl.
  trades.jsonl is SIGNAL/SKIP telemetry: run.py writes a SIGNAL row every loop,
  ~11,500 rows / ~1.7 MB a day. `data collection 1` carried 187,406 of them.
  tools/ledger_export.py filters it to the trade lifecycle and autosync
  publishes that (+ the state files). The raw log stays on the box;
  AUTOSYNC_PUBLISH_EVENT_LOG=1 restores the old behaviour.

autosync: never report a merge that did not happen.
  If `git merge` aborts before writing .git/MERGE_HEAD, the resolver committed
  whatever was staged and reported `deployed`, silently dropping the upstream
  change. It now requires MERGE_HEAD and otherwise rolls back. The rollback also
  restores the tracked event log, which `git reset --hard` was reverting.

Tests: 48 autosync checks (new: an upstream deletion of a tracked log must roll
back, not fake a deploy), 9 check_data, 15 ledger-export. Smoke gate: 13 suites.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
